    "explanation": "To both parties — here is what the record shows and how the money was worked out.\n\nDistance driven. The receipt's 20 km is the meter's rounded copy. The governing measurement is the driver's own device trace, EV-GPS-00112 (trace_distance_km 22.4 km; coverage 100%, 203 fixes, no gaps, 10-second sampling). Measured against the booked optimal of 17.6 km on EV-TRIP-00112, the excess is 4.8 km, or 27.27% of optimal. Both POL-RD-01 tests therefore pass (over 20% and over 0.8 km) and a route deviation is ESTABLISHED. It is NOT a MATERIAL deviation: 4.8 km is below the 8.0 km gate and 27.27% is below the 50% gate, so POL-RD-06 does not engage and no full-refund uplift or driver-conduct referral arises. The trace is clean under POL-RD-07(a)–(c), so no independent corroboration was required.\n\nRider's money claim. POL-RD-02 recomputes the optimal-route fare: (3.80 base + 0.75 x 17.6 km + 0.28 x 28.0 min) x surge 1.0 (read from the fare breakdown, the only artifact that carries it) + 0.80 platform fee = 3.80 + 13.20 + 7.84 + 0.80 = 25.64. That is HIGHER than the 25.26 you were charged. Refund = charged_total less recomputed_fare, floored at zero = 0, which is below the 0.50 de minimis figure. No distance refund is payable and the charge stands. The reason is that the stored optimal duration (28.0 min) exceeds your actual 20.2 min, so the optimal-route fare would have been dearer than what you paid.\n\nRider-requested stop (POL-RD-04). EV-CHAT-00112 shows your 18:33:02 request to pass by Paya Lebar Quarter (waypoint_hint VEN-PAYA-LEBAR-QUARTER) and the driver's \"ok can\" at 18:33:30. But POL-RD-04 exonerates a deviation only where the trace shows the vehicle passing within 200 m of the waypoint, or travelling the named road. The derived trace package answers no such test and the coordinate may not be re-derived, so the excess is not exonerated on this record; it stands as the established deviation above. Nothing turns on it: whether the excess is exonerated as rider-requested or stands unexplained, your refund is nil. The driver's statement that he made the stop and your contrary assertion are both party accounts the trace does not resolve, and no money follows either way.\n\nDriver compensation (POL-RD-04, second limb). The fare breakdown (EV-FARE-00112, distance_charged_km) billed you for 20.0 km while the trace records 22.4 km — a 2.4 km shortfall, above the 1.0 km trigger. The driver is compensated at SGD 0.75 per unbilled kilometre: 0.75 x 2.4 = 1.80, already on the 0.05 grid and well below the 15.00 per-trip cap. Because this is the outcome the case produces, the ruling is DRIVER_COMPENSATION, amount_sgd carries the same figure, and your own charge is left as it stands.\n\nOther clauses. The fare carries no ERP, toll or airport-surcharge line (all 0.00), so POL-RD-09 gives nothing to correct and no billing-integrity referral fires. The only traffic advisory, EV-ADVIS-00112-01, carries no payload, so POL-RD-03 exonerates nothing. The driver's \"the app was acting up\" is not one of the policy's exculpations and does not shorten the distance actually driven. You discharged the POL-RD-08 burden with matching trip identifiers on an unimpaired trace, and rider_profile.deviation_claims_60d is 1, below the 4-claim trigger, so no extra corroboration was demanded. The driver's profile shows 1 flag in 296 completed trips, so no repeat-deviation pattern is established. No POL-RD-10 escalation trigger fired, and no clause produces a referral.",
