    "confidence": 0.9,
    "explanation": "Trip TRP-00112, Tampines (Blk 267 Tampines St 21) to One Raffles Place, evening of 4 Aug 2026. Both parties should read the distance finding and the money finding separately, because they point in opposite directions.\n\nDISTANCE. On the driver's own device trace (EV-GPS-00112) the distance driven was 22.4 km against a booked optimal of 17.6 km (EV-TRIP-00112). POL-RD-01 makes the trace figure, not the trip record's rounded 20.0 km, the governing one, and on the trace the excess is 4.8 km, or 27.3%. Both limbs of POL-RD-01 therefore pass (over 20% and over 0.8 km), so a route deviation is ESTABLISHED. It is not a MATERIAL deviation (below 50% and below 8.0 km), so the POL-RD-06 driver-profile uplift and conduct referral are not engaged; on the profile fields that clause does name, deviation_flags_90d is 1 against 296 completed trips, so no pattern is established either. The trace is clean for policy purposes under POL-RD-07: 203 fixes, 10-second interval, no gap over 90 seconds and no jump over 500 m, so it is neither impaired nor in need of independent corroboration. Nothing exonerates the excess: the only traffic advisory (EV-ADVIS-00112-01) is missing, carrying no payload, so POL-RD-03 cannot run, and the driver's 'the app was acting up' is not one of the policy's exculpations (POL-RD-03/04/05/11/12).\n\nMONEY ON THE RIDER'S CLAIM: ZERO. POL-RD-02 recomputes the optimal-route fare from the fare breakdown (EV-FARE-00112): base 3.80 + 0.75 x 17.6 km (13.20) + 0.28 x 28.0 min (7.84) = 24.84, at the surge multiplier of 1.0 recorded on the fare breakdown, plus the 0.80 platform fee = 25.64 recomputed fare. The rider was charged 25.26. The refund is 25.26 less 25.64, floored at zero, and in any event below the SGD 0.50 de minimis figure, so no distance refund is payable. Your 'I paid for extra distance' point is therefore correct as a fact but yields nothing in arithmetic, because the optimal route's own duration (28 min) exceeds what you actually travelled (20.2 min).\n\nTHE PAYA LEBAR QUARTER STOP. The chat log (EV-CHAT-00112) shows your request at 18:33:02 and the driver's 'ok can' at 18:33:30, and nothing more; it is evidence of the request, not of the stop. POL-RD-04 would exonerate the detour only if the trace placed the vehicle within 200 m of the chat's own waypoint_hint coordinate, and the derived trace package contains no proximity fact, so that corroboration test could not be run and the detour is not exonerated on this record. The driver's counterparty statement that he did make the stop cites no evidence ID and, under POL-RD-08, carries no weight. This contested question does not change the money, because the refund is zero whichever way it goes.\n\nMONEY TO THE DRIVER: SGD 1.80. Separately from the exoneration, POL-RD-04 pays the driver for distance he drove but was not paid for. The fare breakdown bills 20.0 km (EV-FARE-00112) while the governing trace records 22.4 km (EV-GPS-00112) - a shortfall of 2.4 km, above the 1.0 km trigger. At SGD 0.75 per unbilled kilometre that is 2.4 x 0.75 = SGD 1.80, already on the 0.05 grid and far below the SGD 15.00 per-trip cap. That is the only money this case moves, so the ruling value is DRIVER_COMPENSATION; the rider's own charge of SGD 25.26 stands unrefunded. There is no ERP, toll or airport-surcharge line anywhere in the fare, so POL-RD-09 yields nothing and no billing-integrity referral arises.\n\nCONFIDENCE AND OPEN QUESTION. The deciding figures come from the trip record, the GPS trace and the fare breakdown, all admitted at full weight and none impaired, so confidence is 0.9. One question remains open for the human reviewer rather than for the ruling: whether the vehicle in fact passed Paya Lebar Quarter cannot be checked from the derived trace package, and a waypoint-proximity fact in that package would settle it."
